{{smaller|pendicular line of; ''beneath'', close under: as, the sun sinks ''below'' the horizon; a thing is ''under'' a chair or tree, ''beneath'' a pile of rubbish. ''Under'' has often the sense of ''beneath'': as, “''under'' whose wings,” Ruth ii. 12. Compare the old use of ''beneath'' in Ex. xxxii. 19—“''Beneath'' the mount.”}}
{{smaller block|[A sail] that sinks with all we love ''below'' the verge.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, iv.}}
<poem>Whereon a hundred stately beeches grew,
And here and there great hollies ''under'' them.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Pelleas and Ettare.}}
''Beneath'' the milk-white thorn that scents the ev’ning gale.
{{right|''Burns'', Cottar’s Sat. Night.}}}}

