plumed, with a glittering staff of officers at his orders.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Essenes, ii.}}
{{smaller block|With puff’d cheek the ''belted'' hunter blew.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Palace of Art.}}}}
'''2.''' Marked or adorned with a band or circle: as, a ''belted'' stalk; the ''belted'' kingfisher.—'''3.''' Worn in the belt, or hanging from the belt: said especially of a sword the sheath of which is secured permanently to the belt.
{{smaller block|Three men with ''belted'' brands. {{right|''Scott.''}}
He was dressed in his pontifical robes, with a ''belted'' sword at his side. {{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 21.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Belted plaid''', the plaid worn by the Highlanders of Scotland in full military dress: so called from being kept tight to the body by a belt: as, “wi’ ''belted plaids'' and glittering blades,” ''Alex. Laing.''}}

'''Beltein, Belten,''' ''n.'' See ''Beltane.''

'''belting''' (bel′ting), ''n.'' [< ''belt'' + ''-ing.''] Belts collectively or in general; the material of which belts are made. See ''belt.''—{{smaller|'''Angular belting.''' See ''angular.''—'''Round belting''', belting, usually made from a flat strap, which is rolled into a tubular form.—'''Scandinavian belting''', a cotton cloth woven solid and treated with Stockholm tar. ''E. H. Knight.''}}

'''belt-lacing''' (belt′lā″sing), ''n.'' Leather thongs for lacing together the ends of a machine-belt to make it continuous.

'''belt-pipe''' (belt′pīp), ''n.'' In a steam-engine, a steam-pipe surrounding the cylinder.

'''belt-rail''' (belt′rāl), ''n.'' A longitudinal strip or guard of wood along the outside of a street-car, beneath the windows.—{{smaller|'''Belt-rail cap''', a strip of wood fastened to the top of a belt-rail and forming the seat of the window-sill.}}

'''belt-saw''' (belt′sâ), ''n.'' Same as ''band-saw.''

